// en-hal — the pacing of the calm machine.
//
// The variant (!v/hal) can shape the timbre but not the TIMING, and timing is
// the most recognisable thing about this voice. He does not speak quickly and he
// never runs two clauses together; the space around the words is what makes the
// politeness feel considered rather than glib, and it is why the line about
// being afraid lands the way it does.
//
// No phoneme substitutions at all. The performance was Canadian and the vowels
// are General American — there is nothing to change, only to slow down.

name en-hal
language en-hal 8
language en 22

phonemes en

// LONG, AND ALMOST EVEN.
// Default English stretches a stressed syllable well past an unstressed one.
// These are long across the board and the spread between them is small: nothing
// is hurried past, and nothing is leaned on. That evenness is the character —
// a voice that emphasised normally would sound like it had opinions.
stressLength 215 215 220 220 240 240 255 265

// Amplitude barely moves either. He is not louder on the important word,
// because to him there is no important word.
stressAmp 15 15 16 16 17 18 19 20

// THE PAUSE IS THE POINT. A wider gap between words than any other voice in the
// cast. This one line does more for the impression than the whole variant file.
words 4

dictrules 8
